  "text": "A new theoretical description is offered for how ions move into a solid ion‑exchange material, based on the idea of an effective diffusion layer whose concentration changes in a curved way rather than linearly. The resulting formula is broad enough to apply to many different solution concentrations, and it can be simplified to produce the special cases that have been reported previously for ion self‑diffusion in such media. To check the usefulness of this approach, a series of laboratory tests were carried out in which a particular metal ion was allowed to diffuse into a synthetic sulfonated polymer resin from aqueous solutions containing that ion together with a common competing ion. The tests covered a wide span of solution strengths and were repeated at several flow speeds. The measured concentration profiles matched the predictions of the new equation across the entire range, supporting the adequacy of the proposed model."
